# Which pipeline last deployed each Edge Function, read off the project at
# 21:27 UTC on 29 September 2026. Every function, billing-cancel included,
# last deployed by the platform at 21:10:28Z.
#
# Four columns: slug, version, the pipeline that deployed that version, and when.
# `app/src/lib/functionsdeployed.test.ts` holds `supabase/functions/` to this
# reading and explains the rule. A newer reading that disagrees is a finding,
# not a number to update in passing.
#
# ## How the pipeline column is known
#
# It is not a guess or a label somebody chose. The project reports an
# `entrypoint_path` per function, and it names the machine the bundle was built
# on:
#
#     file:///app/supabase/functions/<slug>/index.ts       platform
#     file:///home/runner/work/semester/semester/...        runner
#
# `platform` is Supabase's own deploy off the main branch, which runs on every
# merge. `runner` is `.github/workflows/functions.yml`, which runs only when a
# merge touches that function's directory.
#
# ## The rule, and the failure it is about
#
# **A `runner` row means the platform deploy is not building that function.**
# The platform deploy runs on every merge, so if it were building the function
# it would have overwritten the path by now. A runner path is therefore not a
# harmless detail about provenance; it is positive evidence of exclusion from
# the pipeline that runs continuously.
#
# That is what nobody read for three days. Between **18 September 17:35:25Z**
# and **21 September 22:53:45Z**, `push` and `calendar` sat at v16 and v12 on
# runner paths while the other four accumulated fifteen and sixteen versions
# each. **246 merges landed on main in that window.** Every one of them ran the
# platform deploy, which rebuilt four functions and passed over two, and said
# nothing either time.
#
# The reason neither pipeline reached them is the part worth keeping:
#
#   - the platform deploy skipped them because both still imported over
#     `https://esm.sh/`, which #685 measured against a control and fixed; and
#   - `functions.yml` never fired because **no commit touched either directory
#     in those three days** — checked, not assumed. Its trigger is a change to
#     the directory, and there was none.
#
# So they were not stale. They were unreachable by any deploy, and the last
# deploy either had received was the one that happened to precede the freeze.
# A change merged into `calendar` — the live `.ics` feed — would have gone live
# in the ordinary way, while a change merged anywhere else left it untouched
# and unreported.
#
# ## What a `runner` row does not mean
#
# `functions.yml` has a `workflow_dispatch` escape hatch, and a hand-started
# deploy legitimately writes a runner path. That reading is real but transient:
# the next merge's platform deploy overwrites it. So one `runner` row is a thing
# to look at rather than a fault in itself — **a runner row that survives the
# next merge is the freeze.** Re-read before concluding, which is the same
# instruction `ledger.snapshot` carries and for the same reason.
#
# ## A new function's first row
#
# A new function cannot have a `platform` row yet. The platform deploys only
# what main declares in config.toml, and `functionconfig.test.ts` will not let
# main declare a function until it is live. So a function's first deploy is by
# hand, from its branch (Actions → Deploy Edge Functions, run on that branch),
# and it reads `runner`. Its row carries a fifth column, `first`, which is the
# only thing that excuses a runner row: it says the merge that adds the
# function hands it to the platform. The next reading drops the note, and a
# `first` row that survives that reading is a freeze like any other.
#
# ## Re-reading it
#
# Nothing in a test run can reach the project. The reading comes from the
# Management API's function list (the dashboard's Edge Functions page shows the
# same versions), and the columns below are transcribed from it.

claude            244  platform  2026-09-29T21:10:28Z
